# Tessera stdlib: BorrowedSlice[T] and BorrowedCell[T], memory taken from a manager
#
# `BorrowedSlice[T]` is a run of values and `BorrowedCell[T]` one value, both from a MemoryManager, and each is given back exactly
# once: `drop` gives the memory back, and so does handing the value to a routine that takes a `BorrowedSlice` or a `BorrowedCell` (it
# gives it back then). The block holds the values and nothing else: plain `T` values in a row.
#
# Only a `BorrowedCell[T]` reads and writes its value (`load`, `store`), and only a `@T` its place (`peek`, `poke`). A
# `BorrowedSlice[T]` and a `@[T]` reach their elements the same way: `m[i]` (getitem) is the value, a copy, `m.getslot(i)` is
# its place as a `@T`, and `m.getslice(start, count)` is a run of them as a `@[T]`. An entity element doesn't come out
# as a copy: look at it through `getslot(i)`, or take it out with `steal m.getslot(i)`, which leaves the place hollow.
# Lending is passing a BorrowedSlice where a `@[T]` is expected (it passes `getslice()`, every value) or a BorrowedCell where a `@T`
# is (it passes `getview()`).
#
# A literal names the manager with `from`: `xs: &[U64] <- [0] * n from HEAP` is a BorrowedSlice of n zeros (the count may be
# known only while running), and `node: &Node <- (value: 1, next: .hollow()) from HEAP` a BorrowedCell of one value.
#
# The build checks that memory is given back once, and not used after. A second give-back that code the build doesn't
# follow makes crashes with `DoubleFreeError` under the heap check (`[debug] heap-check`).
#
# `.hollow()` is a BorrowedSlice or a BorrowedCell holding nothing (no address, no manager): `drop` on it does nothing, and a read
# crashes.

module System

### The routine Hosted's AccessManager puts here before it hands out its first access, or hollow: memory given back
### or moved by a BorrowedSlice or a BorrowedCell is handed to it first (its address and size in bytes), and it waits while another flow
### holds an access inside that range. A `Callable[(Addr, U64), None]`.
@target(os: not "none")
global ACCESS_RANGE_CHECK: &Addr

### Hands the block at `at`, `size` bytes, to the AccessManager's check before it is given back or moved, when an
### access was ever handed out.
### :param at: The block.
### :param size: Its size in bytes.
@target(os: not "none")
@inline
secret routine check_access_range(at: Addr, size: U64) -> None
    hook: Addr = ACCESS_RANGE_CHECK.peek_unbroken()
    if hook.is_hollow()
        return
    check: Callable[(Addr, U64), None] = bitcast(hook)
    check.call(at, size)
    return

### Without an operating system there is no AccessManager: nothing to check.
### :param at: The block.
### :param size: Its size in bytes.
@target(os: "none")
@inline
secret routine check_access_range(at: Addr, size: U64) -> None
    return

### Takes room for `count` values of `T` from `manager`.
### :typeparam T: The value type.
### :param count: The number of values, more than 0.
### :param manager: Where the memory comes from.
### :returns: The first value's address, or a hollow address when the manager has none to give (also when the size
###     doesn't fit in U64, which is more memory than any manager has).
@expensive("allocates memory")
secret routine take_block[T](count: U64, manager: @MemoryManager) -> @T
needs T: typename
    if count.mul_overflows(sizeof[T]())
        return .hollow()
    bytes: U64 = sizeof[T]() * count
    # Values of no size still take a block of their own, so the manager has an address to give.
    size: U64 = when
        bytes == 0 => 1
        else => bytes
    taken: Addr = manager.borrow(size, align: alignof[T]())
    return taken.to[@T]()

### Gives the block of `count` values at `data` back to `manager`, once no other flow holds an access inside it.
### :typeparam T: The value type.
### :param data: The first value.
### :param count: The number of values.
### :param manager: Where the memory came from.
@track_caller
@expensive("frees memory")
secret routine give_block[T](data: @T, count: U64, manager: @MemoryManager) -> None
needs T: typename
    check_access_range(at: data, size: sizeof[T]() * count)
    manager.give_back(data)
    return

# == BorrowedSlice[T] ====================================================================================================

### `count` values of `T` from a manager, given back exactly once with `drop`. An entity: never copied, lent as a
### `@BorrowedSlice[T]`. Its elements are reached as a value (`m[i]`), a `@T` (`getslot`), or a `@[T]` (`getslice`).
### :typeparam T: The element type.
entity BorrowedSlice[T]
needs T: typename
obeys ViewingIndexable[BorrowedSlice[T], T]
    data: @T
    count: U64
    manager: @MemoryManager

### `count` uninitialized values of `T` from `manager`, given back with `drop`. A count of 0 takes nothing and keeps
### the manager.
### :param count: The number of values.
### :param manager: Where the memory comes from and goes back to.
### :returns: The memory.
### :throws: Throws `OutOfMemoryError` when the manager has no memory to give, or when the size of `count` values
###     doesn't fit in U64.
@expensive("allocates memory")
failable common routine BorrowedSlice[T].construct(count: U64, manager: @MemoryManager) -> Me
needs T: typename
    if count == 0
        return Me(data: .hollow(), count: 0, manager)
    data: @T = take_block[T](count, manager)
    if data.is_hollow()
        throw OutOfMemoryError()
    return Me(data, count, manager)

### `values` written `times` over, one run after another, in memory from `manager`: what `[a, b] * n at manager`
### makes.
### :param values: The values to repeat.
### :param times: How many times they are written.
### :param manager: Where the memory comes from and goes back to.
### :returns: The memory, `times` runs of `values`.
### :throws: Throws `OutOfMemoryError` when the manager has no memory to give, or when the count or its size doesn't
###     fit in U64.
@track_caller
@expensive("allocates memory")
failable common routine BorrowedSlice[T].construct(values: @[T], times: U64,
    manager: @MemoryManager) -> Me
needs T: typename
    if values.count.mul_overflows(times)
        throw OutOfMemoryError()
    made: &Me <- .hollow()

    count: U64 = values.count * times
    when check Me.construct(count, manager)
        is Me as memory => made.store(steal memory)
    view: @[T] = made.getslice()

    if values.count == 0
        return made
    each i in 0 til times
        view.getslot(i * values.count).copy(src: values.data, count: values.count)
    then
        return made

### The number of values.
### :returns: The count.
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].count() -> U64
needs T: typename
    return me.count.peek()

### The values as a view: what lending passes where a `@[T]` is expected.
### :returns: A span over every value, valid until the memory is given back or resized.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the memory is hollow and holds values.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].getslice() -> @[T]
needs T: typename
    data: @T = me.data.peek()
    count: U64 = me.count.peek()
    if count == 0
        return @[T].construct(data, count: 0)
    if data.is_hollow()
        throw UnacceptedValueError(description: "This memory holds nothing: it is hollow, or was given back.")
    return @[T].construct(data, count)

### The `count` values from `start` as a view.
### :param start: The position of the first value.
### :param count: The number of values.
### :returns: A span over them, valid until the memory is given back or resized.
### :throws: Crashes with `IndexOutOfBoundsError` when the range runs past the end.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].getslice(start: U64, count: U64) -> @[T]
needs T: typename
    return me.getslice().getslice(start, count)

### The value at `index`, a copy. An entity value doesn't come out as a copy: look at it with `getslot(index)`, or
### take it out with `steal m.getslot(index)`.
### :param index: The zero-based position.
### :returns: The value.
### :throws: Throws `IndexOutOfBoundsError` when `index >= count`.
failable routine @BorrowedSlice[T].getitem(index: U64) -> T
needs T: typename
    count: U64 = me.count.peek()
    if index >= count
        throw IndexOutOfBoundsError(index, count)
    value: T = me.getslice().getslot(index).peek()
    return value

### The place of the value at `index`: its address, `data + index * T.data_size()`.
### :param index: The zero-based position.
### :returns: The place, valid until the memory is given back or resized.
### :throws: Crashes with `IndexOutOfBoundsError` when `index >= count`.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].getslot(index: U64) -> @T
needs T: typename
    return me.getslice().getslot(index)

### The address of the first value: for C code that takes the values' address.
### :returns: `data`, hollow for an empty memory.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the memory is hollow and holds values.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].to[@T]() -> @T
needs T: typename
    return me.getslice().data

### Exchanges the values at `first` and `second` in place. Each value is moved, never copied, so entity values swap
### too, and neither place is left hollow. The same index twice changes nothing. Work on another thread that swaps
### elements another flow can reach holds an exclusive access to both first (`m.getslot(i).get_exclusive_access()`),
### taken in address order.
### :param first: The zero-based position of one value.
### :param second: The zero-based position of the other value.
### :throws: Crashes with `IndexOutOfBoundsError` when `first >= count` or `second >= count`.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].swap(first: U64, second: U64) -> None
needs T: typename
    me.getslice().swap(first, second)
    return

### Keeps only the first `count` values in use: no copy and no reallocation, for a buffer filled with less than it
### holds.
### :param count: How many of the values are in use, at most `count()`.
### :throws: Crashes with `IndexOutOfBoundsError` when `count` is more than the memory holds.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].keep_first(count: U64) -> None
needs T: typename
    if count > me.count.peek()
        throw IndexOutOfBoundsError(index: count, count: me.count.peek())
    count.poke_into(me.count)
    return

### A copy in memory of its own from the same manager, given back on its own. The values are copied bit for bit.
### :returns: The copy.
### :throws: Crashes with `OutOfMemoryError` when the manager has no memory to give.
@track_caller
@expensive("allocates memory")
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].duplicate() -> Me
needs T: typename
    view: @[T] = me.getslice()
    copy: &Me <- Me.construct(count: view.count, manager: me.manager.peek())

    if view.count == 0
        return copy
    copy.data.peek().copy(src: view.data, count: view.count)
    return copy

### Stops the program for a resize of hollow memory: a routine that isn't failable, so its throw stops the program
### instead of giving the failure to resize's caller.
@noreturn
@track_caller
secret routine crash_hollow_resize() -> None
    throw UnacceptedValueError(description: "This memory is hollow (it has no manager), so it can't be resized.")

### Holds `count` values from here on, keeping the first ones (as many as both counts allow). The memory may move:
### views and addresses taken before see it no longer. A count of 0 gives the memory back and keeps the manager.
### :param count: The new number of values.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` on a hollow memory (no manager). Throws `OutOfMemoryError` when the
###     manager has no memory to give or the size doesn't fit in U64, and the memory is unchanged then.
@track_caller
@expensive("reallocates memory")
@reshaping
failable routine @BorrowedSlice[T].resize(count: U64) -> None
needs T: typename
    manager: @MemoryManager = me.manager.peek()
    data: @T = me.data.peek()

    if manager.is_hollow()
        crash_hollow_resize()
    if count == 0
        drop me
        me.manager.poke(manager)
        return
    elseif data.is_hollow()
        when check Me.construct(count, manager)
            is Me as memory =>
                me.poke(steal memory)
                return
    else
        check_access_range(at: data, size: sizeof[T]() * me.count.peek())
        if count.mul_overflows(sizeof[T]())
            throw OutOfMemoryError()
        bytes: U64 = sizeof[T]() * count
        # Values of no size keep a block of one byte: a block of none would be given back by the move.
        size: U64 = when
            bytes == 0 => 1
            else => bytes
        moved: @MemoryManager = me.manager.peek()
        grown: Addr = moved.reborrow(at: data, size, align: alignof[T]())
        if grown.is_hollow()
            throw OutOfMemoryError()
        me.data.poke(grown.to[@T]())
        me.count.poke(count)
        return

### Gives the memory back to its manager and leaves the value hollow. Does nothing on a hollow or empty value. The
### values themselves are not destructed: see `destruct_all`.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].destruct() -> None
needs T: typename
    data: @T = me.data.peek()
    if not data.is_hollow()
        give_block[T](data, count: me.count.peek(), manager: me.manager.peek())
    me.poke(.hollow())
    return

### Gives the memory back and puts `value` in its place: how a global that holds memory changes (the program's end
### gives the last one back).
### :param value: The memory that takes its place, taken over.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].replace(value: Me) -> None
needs T: typename
    drop me
    me.poke(steal value)
    return

### Gives the memory back and keeps the manager: it holds nothing afterward, and a `resize` draws from the same manager
### again. What a collection's `destruct` does with its storage. Nothing on a hollow or empty value.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].give_back() -> None
needs T: typename
    manager: @MemoryManager = me.manager.peek()
    drop me
    me.manager.poke(manager)
    return

### Moves the memory out to the caller and leaves this one holding nothing, with the same manager: a `resize` draws
### from it again.
### :returns: The memory that was here, which the caller drops.
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].take() -> Me
needs T: typename
    taken: &Me <- steal me.stride(0)

    me.poke(.hollow())
    me.manager.poke(taken.manager.peek())
    return taken

### Destructs every value, then drops the memory: for values that hold something, such as lists. Every value must be
### initialized.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedSlice[T].destruct_all() -> None
needs T: typename, Destructible[T]
    view: @[T] = me.getslice()

    each i in 0 til view.count
        drop view.getslot(i)
    drop me
    return

# == BorrowedCell[T] =======================================================================================================

### One value of `T` from a manager, given back exactly once with `drop`. An entity: never copied, lent as a `@BorrowedCell[T]`.
### Read with `load`, written with `store`. Lend `getview()` where a `@T` is expected.
### :typeparam T: The value type.
entity BorrowedCell[T]
needs T: typename
    at: @T
    manager: @MemoryManager

### `value`, in memory from `manager`, given back with `drop`.
### :param value: The value, taken over.
### :param manager: Where the memory comes from and goes back to.
### :returns: The box.
### :throws: Crashes with `OutOfMemoryError` when the manager has no memory to give.
@track_caller
@expensive("allocates memory")
common routine BorrowedCell[T].construct(value: T, manager: @MemoryManager) -> Me
needs T: typename
    at: @T = take_block[T](count: 1, manager)
    if at.is_hollow()
        throw OutOfMemoryError()
    at.poke(steal value)
    return Me(at, manager)

### The value's address: what lending passes.
### :returns: The address, valid until the box is dropped.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the box is hollow.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedCell[T].getview() -> @T
needs T: typename
    at: @T = me.at.peek()
    if at.is_hollow()
        throw UnacceptedValueError(description: "This box holds nothing: it is hollow, or was dropped.")
    return at

### The value.
### :returns: A copy of the value.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the box is hollow.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedCell[T].load() -> T
needs T: typename
    return me.getview().peek()

### Writes `value` into the box.
### :param value: The value to write, taken over.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the box is hollow.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedCell[T].store(value: T) -> None
needs T: typename
    me.getview().poke(steal value)
    return

### Writes this value into the box `dest`: `dest.store(me)` read from the value's side, for a chain that ends in the
### box (`x.load().add(1).store_into(x)`).
### :param dest: The box to write to.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the box is hollow.
@track_caller
routine T.store_into(dest: @&T) -> None
needs T: typename
    dest.store(steal me)
    return

### Moves the value out and leaves the place hollow (`.hollow()`): how an element of a `BorrowedSlice[T]` is taken out, an
### entity value included.
### :returns: The value that was there.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the box is hollow.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedCell[T].take() -> T
needs T: typename
    place: @T = me.getview()
    return steal place.stride(0)

### The value in a box of its own from the same manager.
### :returns: The copy.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the box is hollow.
@track_caller
@expensive("allocates memory")
routine @BorrowedCell[T].duplicate() -> Me
needs T: typename
    return Me.construct(value: me.load(), manager: me.manager.peek())

### Gives the memory back to its manager and leaves the box hollow. Does nothing on a hollow box. The value itself
### is not destructed.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedCell[T].destruct() -> None
needs T: typename
    at: @T = me.at.peek()
    if not at.is_hollow()
        give_block[T](data: at, count: 1, manager: me.manager.peek())
    me.poke(.hollow())
    return

### Gives the memory back and puts `value` in its place: how a global that holds a box changes (the program's end
### gives the last one back).
### :param value: The box that takes its place, taken over.
@track_caller
routine @BorrowedCell[T].replace(value: Me) -> None
needs T: typename
    drop me
    me.poke(steal value)
    return
